  "worked_solution": "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }k(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(k\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+{k}^{2}t){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =k\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+{k}^{2}\\pi -k\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-0 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}\\pi  & =0 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
